The rise of large language models (LLMs) has sparked significant interest in their ability to perform reasoning tasks. While LLMs have demonstrated strong performance on standard reasoning benchmarks, they often fail to generalize to novel tasks. This study examines the generalization capabilities of LLMs across three distinct reasoning domains: arithmetic, natural language inference, and commonsense reasoning. Our results show that LLMs struggle to generalize to new tasks within these domains. Furthermore, we identify that the primary cause of this limitation is the models' inability to generalize to new reasoning rules. To address this, we propose a method that enhances the generalization of LLMs to unseen reasoning rules by training them on a small set of in-context examples. We demonstrate that this approach significantly improves generalization performance in all three reasoning domains. Additionally, we find that the effectiveness of this method depends on the specific type of reasoning rule being generalized.

